1
00:00:01,139 --> 00:00:05,440
If you ever deploy the infrastructure manually, you know the feeling.

2
00:00:05,659 --> 00:00:09,060
You click a few buttons and your Kubernetes clusters starts running.

3
00:00:09,220 --> 00:00:12,489
But later on, if you try to repeat the same deployment,

4
00:00:12,659 --> 00:00:14,539
you missed a couple of things here and there.

5
00:00:14,779 --> 00:00:18,500
Infrastructure as a Code exists exactly to prevent that.

6
00:00:18,590 --> 00:00:22,010
Let's see how we can use Terraform to deploy the

7
00:00:22,010 --> 00:00:25,329
clusters and resources into Google Cloud.

8
00:00:25,450 --> 00:00:26,000
For this,

9
00:00:26,129 --> 00:00:30,329
I assume that you already have VS Code installed and in

10
00:00:30,329 --> 00:00:33,150
that you have already added Terraform.

11
00:00:33,450 --> 00:00:34,689
If you're not aware of it,

12
00:00:34,810 --> 00:00:40,690
you simply go down to Google, and it is straightforward to install the VS Code,

13
00:00:40,690 --> 00:00:44,349
and similarly it is straightforward to install Terraform.

14
00:00:44,470 --> 00:00:44,909
Lastly,

15
00:00:44,990 --> 00:00:50,599
I also assume that you have Google SDK also installed on your local machine.

16
00:00:50,750 --> 00:00:52,340
Now I'm using the MacBook,

17
00:00:52,389 --> 00:00:56,069
but this exercise can be easily done on a Windows machine as well.

18
00:00:56,189 --> 00:01:00,310
Now in Terraform, we should have one file, main.tf.

19
00:01:00,509 --> 00:01:02,959
This is to initialize the Terraform configuration.

20
00:01:03,592 --> 00:01:06,222
I start defining by the Google provider.

21
00:01:06,513 --> 00:01:09,572
Then, I'm enabling the required APIs,

22
00:01:09,623 --> 00:01:14,513
and we are creating the VPC network and subnet through this specific code.

23
00:01:14,822 --> 00:01:15,822
Now on similar lines,

24
00:01:15,913 --> 00:01:19,392
I have the variables.tf that contains a couple of

25
00:01:19,392 --> 00:01:23,032
variables where I have given the name, the project ID,

26
00:01:23,313 --> 00:01:23,913
region,

27
00:01:24,032 --> 00:01:29,313
and our network name and our subnetwork name as a variable. And we

28
00:01:29,313 --> 00:01:32,865
have outputs.tf where we have defined the output,

29
00:01:33,396 --> 00:01:33,835
network name,

30
00:01:34,026 --> 00:01:36,555
subnetwork self‑link. And lastly, we have

31
00:01:36,555 --> 00:01:41,835
terraform.tfvars where I'm providing the project ID where

32
00:01:41,835 --> 00:01:44,795
I'm going to exactly create all of these.

33
00:01:44,875 --> 00:01:46,636
It's time to go and run that.

34
00:01:46,875 --> 00:01:50,216
So first thing which we need to do is Google Cloud authentication.

35
00:01:50,316 --> 00:01:51,776
So here in the terminal,

36
00:01:51,875 --> 00:01:55,526
I'm typing gcloud auth application‑default login and say Enter.

37
00:01:57,080 --> 00:02:00,209
This will redirect, and I'll do the Google authentication.

38
00:02:00,360 --> 00:02:04,139
Now, once the authentication has been done, I'm going to run terraform init.

39
00:02:05,389 --> 00:02:06,550
That will initialize.

40
00:02:06,750 --> 00:02:10,190
Then I'm going to run terraform plan. That will give me the

41
00:02:10,190 --> 00:02:11,990
exact plan what you're going to create.

42
00:02:12,070 --> 00:02:15,169
And then finally I'm going to say terraform apply.

43
00:02:15,279 --> 00:02:18,910
Now this is the point where it goes and actually starts

44
00:02:18,910 --> 00:02:21,580
deploying our resources into the Google Cloud.

45
00:02:21,649 --> 00:02:25,919
Now you can see that it has successfully created our resources.

46
00:02:26,039 --> 00:02:31,160
Now I go down to my Google Cloud, go down to my specific project, that is hpc1,

47
00:02:31,399 --> 00:02:34,639
and inside that, if I go down to my VPC there,

48
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:40,789
so you can see that I got it, demo‑vpc‑1. And if I go inside the demo‑vpc‑1,

49
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:46,580
I should be able to see the subnet as well, that is demo‑subnet‑1.

50
00:02:46,779 --> 00:02:51,369
So that's how our Terraform has created the resource into the Google Cloud.

51
00:02:51,500 --> 00:02:54,500
On similar lines, we can also use the CFT.

52
00:02:54,699 --> 00:02:58,220
CFT stands for Google Cloud Foundation Toolkit.

53
00:02:58,419 --> 00:03:02,500
They are the already approved templates from Google which you can

54
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:05,250
use to create your Infrastructure as a Code.

55
00:03:05,350 --> 00:03:10,580
For example, in this, I'm using the CFT network module from the CFT,

56
00:03:10,619 --> 00:03:13,949
which is an approved module for creating the network resources.

57
00:03:14,149 --> 00:03:16,110
So instead of writing your own code,

58
00:03:16,229 --> 00:03:20,419
you can use the pre‑built templates from the Google CFT, and

59
00:03:20,419 --> 00:03:23,750
you can use that for creating your resources.
